  <!-- Section 4: Foundational calculus review and state notation -->
  <paragraphs xml:id="notes-20260826-p4">
    <title>Foundations of rates and linearizaton</title>
    <p>
      <!--
      Transitioning from course logistics to ordinary differential equations requires a deliberate shift in mathematical perspective.
      In traditional college algebra and standard calculus optimization problems, the objective is to solve for a static numerical value or an independent coordinate vector.
      In ordinary differential equations, the unknown element a student seeks to uncover is an entire continuous function trajectory.
      The target trajectory is denoted in this course as the dependent variable state function <m>x(t)</m> operating over an independent time domain <m>t</m>.
   -->
    </p>
    <p>
      Before analyzing complex equations, we must verify foundational rate notation and derivative operations.
      As a baseline mathematical exercise, let us consider the exponential function:
    <example xml:id="example-mod0-rate-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate the instantaneous rate of change of the foundational exponential function:
          <md>x(t) = e^{3t}</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          To isolate the instantaneous rate component with respect to our independent time variable <m>t</m>, we apply the calculus chain rule operation to our candidate expression:
          <md>\frac{dx}{dt} = \frac{d}{dt}\left(e^{3t}\right) = 3e^{3t}</md>
        </p>
        <p>
          We then substitute the original state definition expression directly back into the derivative result:
          <md>\frac{dx}{dt} = 3x</md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The final derivative expression demonstrates an essential relationship: the rate of change of the system is directly proportional to the active state value itself.
          The stated rate balance law forms the literal core of first-order ordinary differential equations.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </paragraphs>

